{"seq":28,"id":"AC3.CK2","type":"check","at":"2026-10-30T14:09:22Z","by":"AC3","prev":"sha256:4e0bae00cab5569b975da5a8e9c26aeaa1dc85a7947d1d5c2365b98ced7d079e","content_hash":"sha256:967a00b0b2129070f7052f062d1a701d5f7b5652587e2c253aa45d19e348af49","supersedes":null,"origin":null,"body":{"target":"H6","outcome":"disputed","extent":"full","notes":"Everything checked holds except one wording point. AC3.RN2 (results AC3.RS7-RS12; own code): multiplicative case from D3/D9 by enumeration: h_b=-2(x^2-2x+p)/((2-x)(1-x)) and h_a=2(p-x^2)/(x(1+x)) (sympy), only root in (0,1) is 1-sqrt(1-p) resp. sqrt(p); with q=sqrt(1-p), p-x*=q(1-q)>0 and sqrt(p)-p=s(1-s)>0; h factorises with sign of (x-x*) (iETS_I) and (x*-x) (iETS_O), so unique and stable. Additive case, my own proof: h_b=2atanh(x)[(1-p)-pR(x)], R=atanh(1-x)/atanh(x) strictly decreasing from +inf to 0, so a unique root with R(x*)=(1-p)/p, h_b<0 left and >0 right (stable for iETS_I; h_a=-h_b gives stable for iETS_O, same root); x*>p iff atanh(1-p)/(1-p)>atanh(p)/p iff p<1/2 (atanh(t)/t increasing); R(1/2)=1 gives x*<1/2 for p<1/2 (mirror for p>1/2). Numerics at 40 digits on 1,999 p plus p=1e-9, 1-1e-9: 0 unreduced identities, 0 counterexamples, root residual <7e-41, one sign change on 2,999 x-points for 80 p. DISPUTE: the claim says 'for every p in (0,1)' the additive root is 'strictly between p and one half'. At p=1/2 the root is exactly 1/2 (AC3.RS10 = 0.5), equal to p, so it is not strictly between; derivations.md and E27 s6 state the exception, the claim statement drops it. Correction: 'strictly between p and one half for p != 1/2, and equal to 1/2 at p = 1/2'. The mathematical content is otherwise confirmed."}}
